# What this repository's own dogfood SAST scan leaves out. Consumers are not
# affected: semgrep reads this file from the scanned root, and every
# module-test and smoke cell scans a fixture directory as its root.
#
# A .semgrepignore *replaces* semgrep's built-in ignore list instead of adding
# to it, so the defaults below are restated. Without them a new file here
# would silently widen the scan -- measured against semgrep 1.162.0, with no
# ignore file it skips every *_test.go and the .git directory, and with an
# ignore file that does not name them it scans both. Every ancestor commit's
# objects under .git are not this tree's code.

# Version control metadata, a directory in a clone and a file in a worktree
# (implicit in semgrep's defaults, lost with them)
.git

# Semgrep's built-in defaults: common large paths
node_modules/
build/
dist/
vendor/
.env/
.venv/
.tox/
*.min.js
.npm/
.yarn/

# Semgrep's built-in defaults: common test paths
test/
tests/
*_test.go

# Semgrep's built-in defaults: rule and log folders
.semgrep
.semgrep_logs/

# Fixture apps, for the same reason trivy.yaml skips them: they carry insecure
# code on purpose (cookies without flags and raw HTML in the DAST target, a
# curl-pipe-shell workflow for the GitHub Actions rule, an unhardened pnpm
# workspace) so the integration tests have something to find. They are test
# data, not this repository's code, and each fixture is still scanned when a
# test points cicd at it. Anchored to the root: the testdata/ directories under
# src/ hold recorded tool output for unit tests and stay in scope.
/testdata/
